Password hygiene

password hygiene is practicing a good set of password habits to ensure that your online accounts is kept safe from black hat
hackers.

there are many practices when it comes to password hygiene. these include: creating long unique password (roughly 12-16
characters long. includes a mix of letters, numbers and symbols), never reuse the same password, using multi factor
authentication, do not under any circumstances share details to anyone (not even family, friends or coworkers), and watch out
for phishing by not trusting strange or fake emails. also make sure it is not easily discoverable. this includes using
passwords based off of personal data or preferences

there are many ways in which a password can get compromised. these can be things such as: credentials stuffing, phishing, data breach,
dictionary attacks, malware, shoulder surfing, and passwod reset abuse. credential stuffing is when an attacker finds a username/password
pair and tries them on multiple sites to try and login on them. dictionary attacks are when hackers use automated guessing via worlists
of common passwords, names, dates and patterns.

